  "query_preview": "..Type IV Monsoon pattern in place today with the \nupper high well to our east and an upper level trof along the \nCalifornia coast. This pattern usually brings an increased \nthreat of severe storms, flash flooding, and nocturnal storms.\n\nStorms a little slower to get going this afternoon, which was not \noverly unexpected with some overnight activity and morning cloud \ncover leading to the delay. PWATs across the area this afternoon \nranged from near 1\" along the AZ/NM border to 1.70\" across western \nPima county. Once storms get going, the main concern will be strong \nto near severe winds, heavy rain with potential rainfall rates with \nsome of the stronger cores being 1-2\"/hr leading to flash flooding, \nespecially west of Tucson, which was hit rather hard last evening. \nFlash flood watch remains in effect until 5 am Monday for Pinal, \nPima and Santa Cruz counties.\n\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery this afternoon showed a weak upper \nlevel short-wave trof along central-CA coast. This feature will \ncontinue to move east over the next 24 hours and extend from ern NV \nsouth to SW AZ. Dynamics from this feature may produce some storms \naround sunrise west of Tucson otherwise looking for another day of \nscattered showers and thunderstorms, with main threats being heavy \nrain and flash flooding, especially west of Tucson. In\nanticipation of heavy rains across the Tohono O'odham nation \ntonight and again on Monday aftn/eve, decided to extend the \nflash flood watch for the nation through 10 pm Monday.\n\nTuesday and Thursday: Low-grade monsoon days as the monsoon \ncalendar turns to September and possible tropical influences \nduring the month.\n\nSpeaking of the tropics, NHC is monitoring a tropical wave with \ndisorganized showers and thunderstorms well south of SW Mexico. This \nmay become the next named eastern Pacific tropical storm late this \nweek with some model guidance (30/12z GFS) indicating that the \npotential track will be further east than where current Tropical \nstorm Karina is at, and possibly closer to Baja by the end this week \ninto next weekend. Confidence low this far out on how much tropical \nmoisture would be advected across the area but something to keep an \neye on.\n\nTemperature wise, below normal highs to start the week then near \nnormal highs Wednesday into next weekend.",
